{"name":"Wood Green","hex":"#a78c59","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Wood Green","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A suburban area and shopping centre in the borough of Haringey, Greater London, England ."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Wood_Green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wood Green","blocks":[{"text":"Wood Green is a suburban district of the London Borough of Haringey, London, England. Its postal district is N22, with parts in N8 and N15. The London Plan identifies it as one of the metropolitan centres in Greater London, and today it forms a major commercial district of north London."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Wood_Green_High_Rd_at_Shopping_City.jpg/330px-Wood_Green_High_Rd_at_Shopping_City.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood_Green","wikidata":"Q1992780"},{"title":"Wood Green (constituency)","blocks":[{"text":"Wood Green was a constituency for the House of Commons of the UK Parliament 1918—1983, centred on the Wood Green area of North London and its earlier broadest form included much of the seat of Enfield Southgate, created in 1950. It returned one Member of Parliament (MP)."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood_Green_(constituency)","wikidata":"Q8032204"},{"title":"Wood","blocks":[{"text":"Wood is a natural, lignocellulosic material derived primarily from the secondary xylem of trees and other woody plants, where it provides mechanical support and in the functional sapwood, conducts water and dissolved minerals. Being a natural material, it is characterized as an organic material – a natural composite of cellulosic fibers that are strong in tension and embedded in a matrix of lignin and hemicelluloses that resists compression."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/16_wood_samples.jpg/330px-16_wood_samples.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood","wikidata":"Q287"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Wood_Green_Crown_Court_1.jpg/500px-Wood_Green_Crown_Court_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Wood_Green_Crown_Court_1.jpg/1280px-Wood_Green_Crown_Court_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Wood_Green_Crown_Court_1.jpg/500px-Wood_Green_Crown_Court_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWood_Gre